sdi-new-sentence 4# 166 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 166 27335 489 52 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 166 28131 502 61 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#166
|  Color-perception has added complexity first because some chromatic
|  variation is expected in an extended region resulting from texture
|  and lighting; and second because visible substances have
|  optical qualities beyond just red/green/blue quantities
|  -=- albedo, specularity, glossiness, metallicity, opaqueness,
|  etc.For instance, [/DavitGigilashvili/], [/XiaoyingGuo/],
|  [/HarveySmithson/], [/Mallickelal/], [/MirjaliliHardeberg/],
|  [/ShinyaNishida/], [/Osadchyetal/], [/WendtFaul/], [/JanaZujovic/].
|  Object boundaries (and meaningful detected edges) are
|  suggested by discontinuities in visual properties of
|  varying kinds, not just pure color; moreover, segmentation
|  algorithms must be wary of spurious boundaries
.  due to light/shadow effects and the vagaries of pigmentation
sdi-sentence-text $# 166 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 166 28132 502 62 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 167 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 167 28135 504 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 167 28661 511 90 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#167
|  Humans subconsciously perform complex image-analysis tasks, many
|  of which can be simulated by computer-vision algorithms [/Galunetal/],
|  [/KrahenbuhlKoltun/],
|  [/TodorovicAhuja/], [/TakeshiUejima/], [/Wolfetal/]:
|  texture segmentation, line/pattern detection, 3D reconstruction, occlusion compensation/interpolation
|  (e.g., perceiving the shape of a vase's back side by projecting
|  around from its visible rim-circle), classifying
.  subregions (in computational parlance, object proposals [/Gavvesetal;for instance/])
sdi-sentence-text $# 167 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 168 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 168 28662 511 91 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 168 28923 515 63 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#168
|  Via color alone we infer textural and optical details, yielding a significantly
|  enlarged color space, and thereby perceive objects' external
|  geometry (e.g., their gradient vectors at each point
.  -=- the degree to which their shape departs from a flat plane)
sdi-sentence-text $# 168 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 169 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 169 28924 515 64 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 169 29043 517 51 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#169
|  We do not consciously direct these processes, by contrast to
.  my example of ascertaining which train is arriving
sdi-sentence-text $# 169 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 170 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 170 29044 517 52 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 170 29316 522 30 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#170
|  Nevertheless,
|  conscious experience is intrinsically structured by
|  faculties of visual (and, to some extent, haptic/tactile)
|  perception so that we can trust our senses as a usually
|  reliable source of information about objects' movement,
.  position, shape, and contours
sdi-sentence-text $# 170 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 171 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 171 29317 522 31 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 171 29436 524 30 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#171
|  We do exercise deliberate
|  control over such data by conscious choices of where to
.  direct attention [/Smithies/]
sdi-sentence-text $# 171 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 171 29437 524 31 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 172 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 172 29439 526 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 172 29584 528 30 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#172
|  Presumably this balance between conscious and subconscious
|  cognition is a fine equipoise, evolved to optimize the
.  efficacy of conscious control
sdi-sentence-text $# 172 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 173 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 173 29585 528 31 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 173 29833 533 14 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#173
|  But even if perceptual
|  processes such as textural segmentation, occlusion compensation,
|  and gradient recognition are not guided by conscious
|  intervention, it is only through consciousness
|  that these factor into larger-scale
.  human actions
sdi-sentence-text $# 173 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 174 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 174 29834 533 15 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 174 30199 540 29 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#174
|  Our minds work in such a manner
|  that pre-conscious operations become consequential
|  as they are woven into conscious experience,
|  against which we orient as perceiver to perceived,
|  whilst also superimposing planning and executive
|  control, situational awareness, personal identity,
|  and our overall commitment to engage the world
.  in rational, purposeful ways
sdi-sentence-text $# 174 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 175 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 175 30200 540 30 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 175 30411 544 59 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#175
|  Consciousness
|  is a medium through which the
|  outputs of cognitive processes acquire the status
|  of perceptual givens, that we deem to disclose
.  states of affairs, relative to which we coordinate actions
sdi-sentence-text $# 175 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 175 30412 544 60 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 176 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 176 30414 546 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 176 30672 550 22 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#176
|  Any comprehensive theory of human cognition, then, is necessarily a
|  theory of how subjective experience serves as a unifying
|  field for preconscious faculties, connecting momentary
|  perceptions to larger-scale goal-directed action and
.  situational reasoning
sdi-sentence-text $# 176 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 177 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 177 30673 550 23 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 177 30989 556 14 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#177
|  Consequently, investigations
|  that abstract away an encompassing first-personal context
|  are only provisional -=- the causal efficacy of a single
|  nerve cell, neuronal cluster, brain region, or
|  functional algorithm replicated via AI, depends on
|  how such entities or processes become manifest in
.  consciousness
sdi-sentence-text $# 177 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 178 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 178 30990 556 15 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 178 31179 559 49 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#178
|  Biologists notice a similar contrast
|  between in vitro and in vivo: molecular
|  interactions in a test tube or device bench versus
.  in living organisms (e.g., [/NancyJNersessian/])
sdi-sentence-text $# 178 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 179 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 179 31180 559 50 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 179 31227 560 29 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#179
|  Lab experiments
.  are simplifying abstractions
sdi-sentence-text $# 179 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 180 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 180 31228 560 30 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 180 31489 565 27 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#180
|  I propose
|  the analogy: consciousness is human reality's in vivo,
|  in that any biological or sociological/economic
|  principles invoked to explain human actions are not explanatorily
|  complete until we consider their presence in
.  first-person consciousness
sdi-sentence-text $# 180 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 180 31490 565 28 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 181 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 181 31492 567 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 181 31670 570 20 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#181
|  Subjective awareness affords a geometric model
|  of surroundings more granular than facts or concepts
|  alone; we can spotlight its functional benefit
.  to living organisms
sdi-sentence-text $# 181 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 182 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 182 31671 570 21 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 182 31847 573 29 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#182
|  Still, the in vivo raises
|  metascientific questions: we cannot dissect, nor neuroimage, animals in
|  nature (the in vitro is artificially
.  staged for data acquisition)
sdi-sentence-text $# 182 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 183 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 183 31848 573 30 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 183 31976 575 59 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#183
|  For
|  consciousness as the humanities' in vivo, analogously one
.  cannot observe another's mental states as objective givens
sdi-sentence-text $# 183 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 184 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 184 31977 575 60 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 184 32026 576 47 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#184
.  This is a well-known philosophical controversy
sdi-sentence-text $# 184 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 185 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 185 32027 576 48 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 185 32192 579 31 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#185
|  Phenomenology
|  offers techniques to adjudicate claims about cognition
|  in consciousness, compensating for the private existence
.  of any one person's experience
sdi-sentence-text $# 185 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 186 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 186 32193 579 32 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 186 32398 582 45 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#186
|  In particular, while introspective
|  consideration of mental states as they occur is imperfect
|  (analysis alters the analyzed), we do have capabilities to
.  reflect on subjective episodes retroactively
sdi-sentence-text $# 186 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 187 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 187 32399 582 46 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 187 32501 584 15 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#187
|  Our minds are primed to learn
|  from experience, and to make inferences about others'
.  mental reality
sdi-sentence-text $# 187 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 188 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 188 32502 584 16 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 188 32701 588 27 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#188
|  Usually this would occur
|  without explicit systematization, perhaps to some
|  extent subconsciously, but it is not a great leap
|  to reflect on past experience with a more
.  analytic/expository intent
sdi-sentence-text $# 188 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 188 32702 588 28 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 189 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 189 32704 590 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 189 32750 590 47 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#189
.  Suppose I examine a colorful glass paperweight
sdi-sentence-text $# 189 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 190 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 190 32751 590 48 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 190 32913 593 5 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#190
|  I will most likely shift my
|  visual focus from place to place once or twice, even when holding the
|  weight still, to discern the coloration on its visible
.  side
sdi-sentence-text $# 190 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 191 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 191 32914 593 6 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 191 32941 593 33 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#191
.  Then I might turn it over
sdi-sentence-text $# 191 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 192 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 192 32942 593 34 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 192 33081 595 35 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#192
|  Perhaps, from touch, I infer that
|  the bottom side has a flattened base, which I now confirm visually
.  while rotating the object manually
sdi-sentence-text $# 192 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 193 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 193 33082 595 36 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 193 33222 597 37 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#193
|  As I just begin that action I
|  have some anticipation of the obverse side's appearance, which (let's
.  say) is borne out by what I see next
sdi-sentence-text $# 193 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 194 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 194 33223 597 38 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 194 33383 599 64 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#194
|  So a proto-image of that
|  hidden side is protained while the top is visible, and when the
.  bottom is seen instead my perception of the top is retained
sdi-sentence-text $# 194 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 195 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 195 33384 599 65 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 195 33459 601 21 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#195
|  The interplay of protention and retention creates a
.  perceptual synthesis
sdi-sentence-text $# 195 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 196 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 196 33460 601 22 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 196 33706 605 27 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#196
|  Validation of protentive expectations
|  plays a logical role in such syntheses, as would sense-data
|  that instead deviate from anticipated -=- say, the paperweight
|  had a plastic or cloth covering on its base, while I
.  presumed it were all glass
sdi-sentence-text $# 196 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 197 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 197 33707 605 28 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 197 33882 608 27 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#197
|  Our ability to both formulate
|  perceptual expectations and adjust to subsequent sense-data
|  gives a rational structure to the minimal exploratory
.  course of sense-perception
sdi-sentence-text $# 197 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 197 33883 608 28 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 198 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 198 33885 610 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 198 34007 612 10 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#198
|  Via discussions like these we can collectively analyze
|  phenomena such as retention, protention, and perceptual
.  synthesis
sdi-sentence-text $# 198 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 199 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 199 34008 612 11 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 199 34097 614 9 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#199
|  Empirical actuality
|  is not required, nor even particularly relevant, for the
.  analysis
sdi-sentence-text $# 199 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 200 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 200 34098 614 10 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 200 34242 616 41 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#200
|  I am not literally holding a paperweight while
|  writing these paragraphs, and a paperweight does not
.  ship with this paper for experimentation
sdi-sentence-text $# 200 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 201 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 201 34243 616 42 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 201 34458 620 33 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#201
|  I assume that
|  both myself and any reader can engage in such hypothetical
|  considerations, and two people may either agree or question
|  each other's use of terms and concepts like
.  protention and retention
sdi-sentence-text $# 201 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 202 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 202 34459 620 34 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 202 34566 622 31 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#202
|  This gives the analysis
|  a public-discursive face, even without empirical
.  science as normally understood
sdi-sentence-text $# 202 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 203 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 203 34567 622 32 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 203 34674 624 45 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#203
|  We aren't using
|  EKG machines to measure neuron activity
.  whilst a test-subject looks at a paperweight
sdi-sentence-text $# 203 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 204 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 204 34675 624 46 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 204 34801 626 52 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#204
|  There may or
|  may not be identifiable neurobiological correlates to the
.  concepts of (e.g.) protention and retention
sdi-sentence-text $# 204 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 205 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 205 34802 626 53 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 205 34946 629 22 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#205
|  Searching for such correlates would be a valid investigation
|  bridging the (metaphorical) in vivo and in vitro
.  facets of human minds
sdi-sentence-text $# 205 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 206 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 206 34953 629 29 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 206 35299 635 21 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#206
|  But as a high-level science,
|  cognitive phenomenology does not need to develop a
|  complete theory about how the parameters in its
|  explanatory arsenal emerge from and/or supervene upon physical substrata
|  (the mind/matter gap is a profound limit to our
|  understanding of the universe, but no more consequential
.  here than elsewhere)
sdi-sentence-text $# 206 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 207 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 207 35300 635 22 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 207 35538 640 22 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#207
|  Its goal, rather, should be to work toward
|  consensus about which theoretical ideas and
|  posits have analytic value and, thereupon, strive for consistency
|  in theory-semantics and in how structural parameters are
.  employed for analysis
sdi-sentence-text $# 207 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 207 35539 640 23 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 208 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 208 35550 642 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 208 35670 644 17 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#208
|  Phenomenologists from Husserl onward have built a
|  literature of analyses and nomenclature toward such
.  theoretical ends
sdi-sentence-text $# 208 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 209 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 209 35671 644 18 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 209 35781 646 9 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#209
|  Phenomenology explores how minimal sensate
|  givens are contextualized in situations and subjective
.  tableaux
sdi-sentence-text $# 209 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 210 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 210 35782 646 10 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 210 35996 650 20 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#210
|  Apperceptive synthesis
|  occurs on multiple time scales, with any momentary perception
|  connected to immediate past and future instants via
|  retention and protention [/GallagherZahavi/], plus
.  memory and planning
sdi-sentence-text $# 210 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 211 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 211 35997 650 21 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 211 36186 654 24 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#211
|  We cannot posit
|  consciousness within single instants -=- the
|  minimal atoms of experience, evidently, are brief
|  but extended perceptual episodes (one or several
.  seconds' duration, say)
sdi-sentence-text $# 211 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 212 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 212 36187 654 25 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 212 36445 658 52 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#212
|  Such episodes then aggregate into
|  larger units whose inner boundaries, we might propose,
|  are defined by deliberate acts of perceptual reorientation
|  (as in the example of switching from watching for an
.  arriving train to focusing toward its window decal)
sdi-sentence-text $# 212 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 213 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 213 36446 658 53 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 213 36595 661 35 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#213
|  And
|  compound episodes chain together into purposeful, situational
|  action, together with the observations and
.  conceptualizations that sustain it
sdi-sentence-text $# 213 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 213 36596 661 36 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 214 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 214 36598 663 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 214 36879 668 14 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#214
|  We therefore have a provisional network of theoretical
|  concepts -=- qualia, proprioception, protention, retention, synthesis,
|  patent/latent, horizon,
|  episodicity, situationality, purposefulness, intentionality
|  -=- to help organize exchanges about conscious states
.  and attitudes
sdi-sentence-text $# 214 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 215 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 215 36880 668 15 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 215 37202 674 17 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#215
|  Phenomenology
|  does not restrict itself to sensory introspections [/DanZahaviChapter;pp. 5, 9, 22/]
|  like this shade of red looks identical to that one;
|  instead, we can reflect on our own experiences to
|  consider how concepts such as intentionality and
|  episodicity form building blocks of rigorous
.  cognitive models
sdi-sentence-text $# 215 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 215 37203 674 18 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 216 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 216 37207 676 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 216 37542 683 13 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#216
|  By systematizing a process
|  for analyzing cognition in-consciousness
|  -=- by analogy to in vivo versus in vitro
|  -=- phenomenology establishes methodological
|  prototypes for humanistic and social sciences in
|  general, at least for that part of their
|  domains where issues of human thought and decision-making
.  are relevant
sdi-sentence-text $# 216 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 217 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 217 37543 683 14 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 217 37733 687 9 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#217
|  Phenomenological terminology is
|  central to such goals; its mode of analysis is not
|  open-ended, but grounded on the deployment of
|  specific ideas with relatively fixed technical
.  meanings
sdi-sentence-text $# 217 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 218 ;.
sdi-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 218 37734 687 10 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 218 37854 689 26 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#218
|  Via this sense of discursive praxis I
|  will argue for connections between phenomenology
.  and cognitive linguistics
sdi-sentence-text $# 218 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-pos 4#/4 218 37855 689 27 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 219 ;.
sdi-sentence-start-pos 4#/4 219 37865 694 1 ;.
sdi-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 219 38173 698 67 ;.
sdi-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#219
|  Similar to the controversial status of first-person reports
|   theoretical confirmability/falsifiability, the
|  linguistics community is divided over the value of
|  analyses based on subjective assessments of
.  acceptability [/EranAsoulin;pp. 52--55/], [/JoanBresnan;pp. 2--6/]
sdi-sentence-text $# 219 ;.

sdi-new-sentence 4# 220 ;.
